# A built Rust tree is far larger than its source, and the whole build
# context is uploaded to the builder. Without this, every deploy ships
# target/ across the wire.
operator/target/
target/
.git/
.github/

# Secrets, and the reason this list is not simply .gitignore:
#
# `COPY . .` uploads the context to whichever local or remote BuildKit
# builder runs the build, and caches it there. Docker does not read
# .gitignore, so a file excluded from git is NOT thereby excluded from
# disclosure — and .gitignore's `*.env` made that easy to miss, because
# the pattern reads as handled.
#
# These two lines cover what .gitignore covers today. They are not a
# mirror of it and must not be maintained as one: `!*.env.example` is
# deliberately absent, because an example file is harmless in a build
# context and reproducing a negation is a way to get the pattern subtly
# wrong for no benefit.
.env
*.env

# Forward-looking, and matches nothing in this repo today. Kept anyway
# because this repo deals in signing seeds — the operator's own, and the
# discovery provider's on the branch that adds publishing tooling — and
# a seed that reaches a cached build context has been disclosed before
# anyone notices the file is new. A deny-pattern that costs nothing is
# the right shape for that; a pattern added after the first mistake is
# not.
*.seed
